Frost Bank customer information was exposed through a cybersecurity incident involving Sefas Innovation, a third-party software and support provider. Unauthorized access occurred within a Sefas-operated SFTP environment used for software support, where files containing Frost customer information were accessed and downloaded. Frost stated that its own systems and networks were not compromised and that banking operations were not disrupted. The incident is significant because sensitive financial and identity data existed outside Frost’s direct security boundary, demonstrating how third-party support infrastructure can become part of a financial institution’s effective attack surface. Publicly disclosed information included names, addresses, Social Security or taxpayer identification numbers, account information, dates of birth, and loan-related data. The exact initial-access method and any specific vulnerability remain publicly unconfirmed.
